It is no secret that we (Hans and Mikael) like to play with graphics and for a
while we had it on our agenda to implement some three-dimensional graphic support
as a follow-up on surface plots. We knew that eventually we had to look at
z-buffering, but never found time to dive into resources. Much of that is not new
but dates from the previous century.

    Is it possible to slap a working \CONTEXT\ module together that does what one
    needs urgently? Imagine we are in a hurry and don't aim for a high-quality
    module.

    Similarly, there is no need for it to be perfect. One can just look at the
    outcome and see if it looks right. No other user has to be exposed so the
    interface can be suboptimal.

    When we look at the code, will it actually be any good? To what extent does
    it conform to the way we like to see code in \CONTEXT\ and its interfacing?

    What is needed to go from an \LLM-generated prototype to a real
    module that we can install?
    The first thing the bot told us is that this is a typical six-month project!

    Does using an \LLM\ (with associated tools and resources) save time or does
    it actually waste time? Can we, with our experience, do it better and faster?

    Will we again follow this route (a quick and dirty solution, stepwise improved,
    eventually okay) or will we work as before?

    The most important question is: do we like it and does it fit in our playing
    with \TEX\ and friends. Is it rewarding at all?

